---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { B } from '../lib/shop';
import { edge, TTL_STATIC } from '../lib/cache';
edge(Astro.response, TTL_STATIC);
---

<Base
  title="How it is made"
  description={`How a ${B.brand} print is made: the artwork is digitally created and edited, then printed on ${B.fulfilment.paper} and framed by hand in ${B.fulfilment.made_in} after you order.`}
>
  <div class="wrap page narrow">
    <h1>How it is made</h1>

    <h2>1. The artwork</h2>
    <p class="prose">
      Each design starts as a digitally created image — generated with image
      software, not painted. We then do the part that decides whether it is
      worth selling: check the subject is actually what it claims to be,
      correct the tone and colour, crop it square to the frame, and discard
      the ones that did not come out. A large share never make it to the
      shop.
    </p>
    <p class="prose">
      We also check what is <em>in</em> the picture. No recognisable people.
      No characters, logos, badges or artwork belonging to anyone else. No
      invented signatures — if the software scrawls something that looks
      like one, that design is cropped or dropped.
    </p>

    <h2>2. The print</h2>
    <p class="prose">
      Printed to order in {B.fulfilment.made_in} on heavyweight matte art
      paper. Matte rather than gloss because a framed print behind glazing
      picks up every reflection, and gloss paper doubles them.
    </p>

    <h2>3. The frame</h2>
    <p class="prose">
      A slim wooden moulding in black, white or natural oak, glazed at the
      front. The hanging fixture is fitted before it is packed, so there is
      nothing to assemble and nothing to buy separately.
    </p>

    <h2>4. The packing</h2>
    <p class="prose">
      Corner-protected and sandwiched in rigid board. If it still arrives
      damaged, send a photograph and we remake it at our cost — see
      {' '}<a href="/returns">returns</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Why made to order</h2>
    <p class="prose">
      Because the alternative is guessing which thousand designs to keep in
      a warehouse, and being wrong. Making each one after it sells costs you
      a few days and means we can offer a far wider range than a shop
      holding stock. It also means nothing is ever "out of stock" — if it is
      in the shop, we will make it.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
